## Why This Is Asked

This task tests designing a runtime-extensible routing layer that remains decoupled from concrete backends, enabling hot-swapping adapters and policies in high-throughput streaming pipelines applicable to modern platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Runtime extensibility via registries and adapters
- Policy-driven routing for load balancing and backpressure
- Exactly-once or at-least-once delivery guarantees with idempotency

## Code Example

```javascript
interface BackendAdapter {
  enqueue(task: any): Promise<void>;
  ack(id: string): Promise<void>;
}
class BackendRegistry {
  private map = new Map<string, BackendAdapter>();
  register(name: string, adapter: BackendAdapter) { this.map.set(name, adapter); }
  get(name: string): BackendAdapter { return this.map.get(name)!; }
}
class Router {
  constructor(private registry: BackendRegistry, private routeFn: (task: any) => string) {}
  async route(task: any) {
    const backendName = this.routeFn(task);
    const adapter = this.registry.get(backendName);
    return adapter.enqueue(task);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement backpressure signals across multiple backends?
- What are the trade-offs of at-least-once vs exactly-once in this design?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design pluggable runtime extensions, handle concurrency considerations, and understand trade-offs between in-process versus out-of-process sinks.

## Key Concepts

- Plugin registry and dynamic loading at runtime
- Minimal interface contracts for telemetry sinks
- Backpressure, batching, and failure isolation
- Stateless versus stateful sinks and lifecycle management

## Code Example

```javascript
interface TelemetrySink {
  send(event: TelemetryEvent): Promise<void>;
  flush?(): Promise<void>;
  shutdown?(): Promise<void>;
}

class PluginRegistry {
  #sinks = new Map();

  register(name, factory) {
    this.#sinks.set(name, factory(this));
  }

  get(name) {
    return this.#sinks.get(name);
  }

  list() {
    return Array.from(this.#sinks.keys());
  }
}

class SyslogSink {
  #buffer = [];
  #batchSize = 100;
  #flushInterval = 5000;
  #paused = false;
  #controller = new AbortController();

  constructor(options = {}) {
    this.#batchSize = options.batchSize ?? 100;
    this.#flushInterval = options.flushInterval ?? 5000;
    this.#startFlushTimer();
  }

  async send(event) {
    if (this.#paused) {
      throw new Error('Backpressure: sink is paused');
    }

    this.#buffer.push(event);

    if (this.#buffer.length >= this.#batchSize) {
      await this.flush();
    }

    if (this.#buffer.length > this.#batchSize * 0.8) {
      this.#paused = true;
    }
  }

  async flush() {
    if (this.#buffer.length === 0) return;

    const batch = this.#buffer.splice(0, this.#batchSize);
    try {
      await this.#sendToSyslog(batch);
      this.#paused = false;
    } catch (err) {
      this.#buffer.unshift(...batch);
      throw err;
    }
  }

  async shutdown() {
    this.#controller.abort();
    await this.flush();
  }

  #startFlushTimer() {
    setInterval(() => this.flush(), this.#flushInterval);
  }

  async #sendToSyslog(batch) {
    // Send batch to syslog endpoint
  }
}

// Usage
const registry = new PluginRegistry();
registry.register('syslog', () => new SyslogSink({ batchSize: 50 }));
```

## Why This Is Asked
A runtime-extendable transformation system tests open-closed principle, type-safety, and runtime composition without touching orchestration.

## Key Concepts
- Registry: maps names to transformation factories.
- Strategy: each transformation is a pluggable strategy.
- Pipeline: sequences transformations into a single pass.
- Runtime extensibility: add new steps without modifying core.
- Type-safety: leverage generics for input/output consistency.

## Code Example
```typescript
interface Transformation<T> { apply(input: T): T; }

type Factory<T> = () => Transformation<T>;

class Pipeline<T> {
  constructor(private steps: Transformation<T>[]) {}
  run(input: T): T {
    return this.steps.reduce((acc, s) => s.apply(acc), input);
  }
}

const registry = new Map<string, Factory<any>>();

function register<T>(name: string, factory: Factory<T>) {
  registry.set(name, factory);
}

function create<T>(name: string): Transformation<T> {
  const f = registry.get(name);
  if (!f) throw new Error(`Unknown transformation: ${name}`);
  return f();
}
```

